text:
maria: Quick heads-up — did a quick SR/keyboard pass on the new toast (top-right) pattern. A few issues I hit: contrast on the close X, focus goes to body after dismiss, and screen reader announces the content twice sometimes.

jason: thanks — can you paste exact repro steps? which browser/AT?

maria: repro: Chrome+NVDA, open toast, press TAB to reach close button, hit Enter to dismiss. Focus lands on page body, NVDA reads toast text again like it's 'live' + manual announce. Also noticed the X icon is #8a8f98 on white => low contrast.

olivia: hmm. we added aria-live="polite" on the toast container to support push messages. maybe the explicit announce + live region overlap.

a11y-bot: Automated contrast check: .toast .close-icon color #8a8f98 vs background #ffffff => contrast ratio 2.8:1 (FAIL, recommended 3:1 for UI components).

leo: my gut: for toasts we should use aria-live="polite" on container and avoid programmatic focus unless it's critical. If we must provide a keyboard focus target (close) then ensure focus returns to the element that opened the toast (or a sensible control), not body.

sam: agreed. also need visible focus ring for the X — current outline is removed in CSS.

maria: proposed fixes: 1) bump close icon color to #2f3a45 (contrast 7.1:1) 2) restore focus-visible style for .toast button 3) on dismiss, focus -> document.activeElementBeforeToast or fallback to nearest actionable control, not body 4) only use programmatic aria-live when toast isn't user-triggered.

jason: can you sketch the CSS and small JS for the focus restore? paste quick snippet and i'll add to PR.

maria: sure — CSS first:

```css
.toast .close-icon {
  color: #2f3a45; /* meets contrast */
}
.toast .close-button:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid #ffb74d; /* distinct visible ring */
  outline-offset: 2px;
  border-radius: 4px;
}
```

and JS pseudocode for focus restore:

```js
// when showing toast
const opener = document.activeElement;
showToast({onClose: () => {
  if (opener && document.contains(opener)) opener.focus();
  else focusNearestActionable();
}});
```

olivia: for aria-live handling: can we add data-trigger="user" vs "system" and set aria-live="polite" only for system ones? user-triggered toasts should not re-announce on dismiss.

maria: yep that's what I meant by programmatic vs user-triggered. Also add role="status" for polite live regions so AT understands it. For assertive we should be very careful.

leo: what about screen reader double announce? that might be because NVDA reads the live region on add, then our dismiss handler re-inserts content for animation end. We should avoid reflowing the innerText on dismiss. Also ensure toast removal happens after animationend and we don't set innerHTML again.

sam: small nit — keyboard order: pressing TAB cycles through focusable controls in top-right then jumps to address bar on mac/chrome sometimes. Could be because toasts are appended at end of DOM. Maybe we should render toasts in a portal near body root but with aria-hidden when not visible to avoid tab stops.

jason: good catch. Implementation plan: keep toasts in portal root, set aria-hidden="true" when not shown; when shown aria-hidden=false and set a data-toast-id on opener to support focus restore. Also ensure close buttons have tabindex=0 (native button is fine).

maria: I'll put together a short PR with CSS + small JS helpers and a11y notes. TL;DR for PR description: "Fix toast accessibility: improve close icon contrast, restore focus-visible, reliable focus restore on dismiss, avoid duplicate SR announcements by separating user vs system toasts and minimizing DOM reflows."

olivia: thanks — also add tests: axe-core scan for contrast + tabbable order + role/status presence.

a11y-bot: Pre-merge checks queued: contrast, tabbable-order, aria-live-role.

jason: perf question — focus restore memory wise we store opener ref; ok for ephemeral UI. Just ensure we null it after close.

maria: will do. Anything else?

leo: one more: add visually-hidden sr-only text inside toast that marks "notification" for screen readers, e.g. <span class=\"sr-only\">Notification:</span> before content so SR users get context. Use CSS .sr-only pattern.

sam: love that. Please include example markup in the PR.

maria: cool — i'll open PR in ~30m. tagging @jason for review. :raised_hands:
